BGIN AVIS: Extensive Comment Language Development Guide (Page 2)
This section defines the "BGIN AVIS Neural Metadata" syntax. In 2026, comments are more than documentation. They are the structured instruction layer that Gemini 1.5 and Vertex AI use to map the FIRE-GEM shared memory.
1. The Metadata Block Structure
Every AVIS-compliant source file must begin with a Reflective Header. This allows the AVIS Coordinator to parse the "intent" of the binary before execution.
asm
; ============================================================================
; @AVIS_METADATA_START
; ID: SN2-PROT-02
; TARGET: FIRE-GEM (0xF17E6E3)
; SYSCALL: 0xBE614
; OFFSET_RULE: MZ_OFFSET=64
; @AVIS_METADATA_END
; ============================================================================
Use code with caution.

2. In-Line "Neural Ingestion" Tags
These specific comment tags signal to Copilot and Vertex AI how to handle memory fragments during the BGIN Pulse.
@INGEST_POINT: Marks a variable or register as a destination for graph-node data.
@PULSE_SYNC: Identifies logic that must be synchronized with the bgin_pulse counter.
@MMAP_GUARD: Warns the AI to apply lock xadd or SYNC keywords to prevent race conditions.
Example implementation in ASM:
nasm
    mov rdi, [fire_gem_ptr]   ; @INGEST_POINT: Target shared map
    add rdi, 0x40             ; @AVIS_OFFSET: Jump to GRAPH_PAYLOAD
    inc dword [rdi - 28]      ; @PULSE_SYNC: Increment bgin_pulse
Use code with caution.

3. The "Fire End" Post-Mortem Tag
Every cleanup routine must be tagged to ensure the fire_end.sh script knows which memory addresses to wipe during a crash or exit.
bash
# @AVIS_FIRE_END_HOOK
# TARGET_SHM: /dev/shm/robot_map
# TARGET_PROC: sentinel-v2
Use code with caution.

4. Extensive Documentation Levels
For 2026 AI ingestion, a three-tier comment system provides "semantic baggage" that helps models avoid hallucinations:
Level 1: Functional (;!): Direct instruction for the assembler.
Level 2: Structural (;#): Defines how this code interacts with the BGIN Lake.
Level 3: Neural (;@): Theoretical context for Gemini's predictive analysis of bottlenecks.
5. Training Copilot: The "Why" Comment
When prompting Copilot to build the language, force it to add a "Rationale Comment" to every generated block. This creates a durable reasoning trail (similar to the Beads framework) that humans and agents can audit later. 
Beyond Vibe Coding
Beyond Vibe Coding
Copilot Directive: "For every block of BGIN AVIS generated, append a ;@RATIONALE comment explaining how this code maintains the SN2-PROT-02 signature."
